<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">El Greco</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1541">1541</a>–<a href="/years/1614">1614</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Domenikos Theotokopoulos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">El Greco, born Domenikos Theotokopoulos at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Candia</a> in Venetian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, was a painter who trained in the Cretan school of icon painting before working in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. From 1577 he settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, where he produced his greatest works, including The Burial of the Count of Orgaz. His elongated figures, vivid colours and visionary religious intensity made him one of the most individual artists of the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/el-greco.jpg" alt="El Greco" width="320" height="354" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: El Greco · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_Greco_-_Portrait_of_a_Man_-_WGA10554.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
